# __Service__ node for n8n

This project defines an n8n node as typed action contracts with `@n8n/node-sdk` (`defineNode`, `t`).
One action is one operation, e.g. `__service__.item.getAll`. n8n turns each action into a node type.
This file is the complete API reference. You do not have to read the SDK source.

## Layout

- `src/index.ts` exports `node` and `actions` (array). Keep these names.
- `src/__slug__.node.ts`: `defineNode` and its resources. `src/credentials.ts`: the credential types.
- `src/actions/<resource>.<operation>.ts`: one action for each file, in kebab case, e.g. the action
  `__service__.item.getAll` is in `src/actions/item.get-all.ts`. `check` fails for another file name.
  Add each action to `actions`.
- `src/actions/<resource>.<operation>.test.ts`: unit tests with `node:test`, `runAction` and `mockHttp`.
- `src/actions/item.get-all.ts` and its test are an example: a declarative list with cursor pages. Rename or replace them.

## Commands

```sh
n8n-node-next check                      # tsc --strict, contract checks and lint
n8n-node-next test                       # run src/**/*.test.ts; a test fails after 20 s
n8n-node-next describe [actionId]        # the typed module the AI builder sees
n8n-node-next run __service__.item.getAll --input '{"paging":{"mode":"limit","max":5}}' --credential-env __ENV__
```

`run` calls the live API and prints the items. `--credential-env __ENV__` reads the field `apiKey`
from `__ENV___API_KEY`. `--credential-file f.json` reads `{ "type": "...", "data": { ... } }`.
After each change, run `n8n-node-next check && n8n-node-next test && n8n-node-next run ...` in one command. `check` runs `tsc`.

## Node and credential

```ts
// src/__slug__.node.ts. The one credential: the types a user may pick, and the scopes actions may need.
export const node = defineNode({ id: '__service__', displayName: '__Service__', baseUrl: 'https://api.example.com/v1',
	credential: credential({ types: [__service__Api], scopes: { 'items:write': 'Create items' } }) }); // `optional: true`: the API also works without a key; a run without one sends no auth
// A resource groups actions. Its `input` (optional) goes into the input of each of its actions.
export const item = node.resource('item', { input: { workspace: t.str().title('Workspace') } });
// src/credentials.ts: `defineCredential`, `credential` and `field` come from '@n8n/node-sdk/credentials'. Id `service.scheme`. auth is data: a.bearer/header/query/basic/apply/when/oauth2.*; a.custom({ reason, sign }) last.
export const __service__Api = defineCredential({ id: '__service__.apiKey', legacyName: '__service__Api', version: '1.0.0',
	displayName: '__Service__ API', fields: { apiKey: field.secret('API Key') }, baseUrl: 'https://api.example.com/v1',
	auth: (a) => a.header('X-Api-Key', '{apiKey}'), test: { get: '/me' } }); // a typo in {apiKey} fails tsc
// `test` needs the credential `baseUrl`, which overrides the node `baseUrl` for this credential: 'https://…', 'http://localhost:<port>…', 'http://127.0.0.1:<port>…' or '{field}'.
```

## Action

```ts
// src/actions/item.create.ts; the id is `__service__.item.create`
export const createItem = item.action('create', {
	action: 'Create an item',               // the label users pick
	summary: 'Create one item.',            // one sentence, at most 120 characters, ends with a period
	flow: { effect: 'write', cardinality: 'per-item', idempotent: false },
	scopes: ['items:write'],                // scopes of the node's credential; tsc rejects others
	input: { name: t.str().title('Name'), ownerId: t.str().title('Owner ID').optional() },  // run() also gets `workspace`
	output: itemOutput,
	async run({ input, http }) {
		const body = { name: input.name, ...(input.ownerId ? { ownerId: input.ownerId } : {}) };
		const response = await http.request({ method: 'POST', path: path`/${input.workspace}/items`, body });
		return parse(itemOutput, response); // passes the body on; n8n checks it against `output`. Fits only a `t.loose` output
	},
});
```

- The action id is `<node>.<resource>.<operation>`. `node.action('ping', {...})` has no resource: `__service__.ping`.
- `flow.effect`: `read` | `write` | `transform`. `flow.cardinality`: `per-item` (one output item per
  input item) or `1:N` (a list). `idempotent?: boolean`.
- `run()` runs once per input item. A `per-item` action returns its output item. A `1:N` action is an
  `async *run()` generator: `yield` each output item, or `yield* pages(...)`. The types reject a
  `per-item` generator and a `1:N` action that returns. n8n checks each item against `output` when it
  is returned or yielded, and pairs it with its input item.
- In `run()`, a field with `.default(v)` is always set, also inside objects. Do not add `??` fallbacks.
  An `.optional()` field can be `undefined`.
- To fail an item, throw. Do not catch errors to return `{ error }`: with "Continue on fail", n8n turns
  the thrown error into an `{ error: message }` item (or sends it to the error output) and runs the next item.
- No code for a simple action: replace `run` with `request: { method: 'GET', path: '/items/{id}' }` (`{id}` is
  the input field; `query`/`body` values are literals or `{ input: 'field' }`; `items: 'results'` for `1:N`).
- When the output shape depends on an input, declare all shapes and pick one in `deriveOutput`:
  `output: t.union(invoice, line)`, `deriveOutput: (input) => (input.split ? line.json : invoice.json)`.
- Never read credentials in `run()`. The credential applies to each request.

## Schemas

| Builder | TypeScript type | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `t.str()` `t.num()` `t.int()` `t.bool()` | `string` `number` `number` `boolean` | `t.int` allows integers only |
| `t.date()` `t.dateTime()` `t.uri()` `t.email()` `t.uuid()` | `string` | n8n checks the format, e.g. `2026-09-15T09:30:00Z` |
| `t.oneOf('a', 'b')` | `'a' \| 'b'` | a fixed list of options |
| `t.lit('a')` | `'a'` | one fixed value |
| `t.nullable(s)` | `T \| null` | for output fields the API sets to `null` |
| `t.arr(s)` | `readonly T[]` | |
| `t.obj({ k: s })` | `{ k: T }` | rejects unlisted fields |
| `t.record(s)` | `Record<string, T>` | any keys, checked values |
| `t.json()` | `Record<string, unknown>` | any object, not checked |
| `t.variant('mode', { a: {...}, b: {...} })` | tagged union | fields that depend on a choice |
| `t.union(a, b)` | `A \| B` | a value that matches one of the schemas |

- Every input field, also nested, needs `.title(text)`: the label n8n shows, e.g. `Owner ID`. Chain `.optional()`, `.default(v)` (also optional for callers), `.describe(text)`,
  `.hint(text)` (at most 80 characters, no period, more than the type), `.with({ pattern, minimum, maximum, minLength, format, examples })`.
- `t.obj` rejects fields it does not list. To return an API object unchanged, list all of its fields,
  or keep unlisted fields with `t.obj({ ... }).with({ additionalProperties: true })`.
- Every `examples` value must match its schema. Do not make a field conditionally required: use `t.variant`.
- `t.variant('kind', { text: { text: t.str() }, json: { data: t.json() } })` is
  `{ kind: 'text'; text: string } | { kind: 'json'; data: Record<string, unknown> }`. A branch `{}` has only the tag.
  Narrow it in `run()` with `if (input.body.kind === 'text') input.body.text`, or a `switch` on the tag.
- `parse(schema, value)` never throws: it passes the value on, typed with each object field optional and
  nullable, so `tsc` makes a read check for a missing field. n8n checks each output item against `output`:
  a mismatch passes on with one warning per run, and fails in `runAction` and fixture tests. `t.loose(t.obj(...))` makes the fields of an API object optional and nullable. `matches(schema, value)` is a type guard. With a strict `t.obj` output, `return parse(...)` fails `tsc`: use `if (!matches(output, response)) throw new OperationalError('...'); return response;`. `Infer<typeof schema>` is the TypeScript type. `isRecord(v)` and `list(v)` narrow `unknown`.

## HTTP

- `http.request({ method, path, url, query, headers, body, fullResponse, timeoutMs, retry })`. Give `path` (starts with `/`, goes after `baseUrl`) or `url` (absolute, e.g. a `next` link), not both.
  Build each `path` with the `path` tag, also a fixed one: ``path`/items/${input.id}` `` encodes each value as one segment. `tsc` rejects a plain string or template.
  A `query` value that is `undefined` is not sent; an array repeats the key (`id=a&id=b`). `body` is sent as JSON. It returns the parsed JSON body as `unknown`: narrow it with `parse`. `timeoutMs` is 300000 when omitted.
- `fullResponse: true` returns `{ body, headers, statusCode }` as `unknown`. Header names are lower case:
  `const full = t.obj({ body: t.arr(item), headers: t.json() }).with({ additionalProperties: true });`
  `if (!matches(full, response)) throw ...;` then `response.body` is typed; check `typeof response.headers.link`.
- A non-2xx response throws. After `isHttpError(error)`, read `error.status`, `error.body` and
  `error.headers` (lower case, e.g. `error.headers['retry-after']`). Throw `UserError` (the user can fix the cause) or `OperationalError` (a retry can pass) from `@n8n/node-sdk`; `check` rejects `new Error(...)`.
- Do not write retries. n8n retries a 429, 502, 503, 504 or a dropped connection up to 3 times, with
  backoff and `Retry-After`, for GET, HEAD, PUT, and every request of an action with `flow.idempotent: true`.
  `retry: true` also retries another method; `retry: false` never retries.
- Lists: `list: { path, query?, response, items(page, input), pages? }` replaces `run()` (see
  `src/actions/item.get-all.ts`); `pages` adds `paging`, and n8n checks each page against `response`. `pages` is `{ style: 'cursor', next, send, size? }`,
  `{ style: 'link', size: { query: 'per_page', max: 100 } }` (follows `rel="next"` of the `Link` header), or `{ style: 'offset', unit: 'item' | 'page', send: { query: 'offset' }, size? }` (a short page ends the list).
  In `run()`, use `pages` and `paging` (`limitOf`, `pages`, `paging` come from `@n8n/node-sdk`). Do not write a page loop. One run may send 10000 requests.
  ```ts
  input: { query: t.str().title('Query'), paging },  // `{ mode: 'all' }` or `{ mode: 'limit', max }`
  async *run({ input, http }) {
  	yield* pages(http, {
  		page: t.obj({ items: t.arr(itemOutput), nextCursor: t.nullable(t.str()) }),
  		request: (cursor, room) => ({ path: path`/items`, query: { q: input.query, cursor, pageSize: Math.min(room ?? 100, 100) } }), // room: limit minus items so far; undefined for all
  		items: (page) => page.items, next: (page) => page.nextCursor, limit: limitOf(input.paging),
  	});
  },
  ```

## Resource locator

```ts
// A `ref(resource)` input field gets a search list, an ID box and a URL box in n8n. `defineResource`, `ref`: '@n8n/node-sdk'.
// src/__slug__.node.ts. `list` is data, not code: n8n sends the lookup request with the credential.
export const folder = defineResource({ id: '__service__.folder', label: 'Folder',
	shape: { pattern: '^(fld_[a-z0-9]+|https://.+)$' }, // the run gets a URL as it is, so the shape takes it too
	extract: '/folders/(fld_[a-z0-9]+)',                // the first group is the ID in a URL; this adds the URL mode
	list: {
		request: { path: '/folders', query: { q: { input: 'search' } } }, // `search` is the text the user types
		response: t.obj({ folders: t.arr(t.obj({ id: t.str(), name: t.str() })), nextCursor: t.nullable(t.str()) }),
		items: 'folders', item: { id: (f) => f.id, label: (f) => f.name, url: (f) => `https://app.example.com/folders/${f.id}` }, // or '={{ $json.name }}'
		pages: { style: 'cursor', next: 'nextCursor', send: { query: 'cursor' }, size: { query: 'pageSize', max: 100 } },
		search: 'service', // or 'label': n8n keeps the entries whose label holds the text
	} });
// In an action: input: { folder: ref(folder).title('Folder') }, and in run():
const folderId = /\/folders\/(fld_[a-z0-9]+)/.exec(input.folder)?.[1] ?? input.folder;
```

## Tests

```ts
const fetch = mockHttp([
	{ method: 'POST', path: '/items', reply: { status: 201, json: { id: 'itm_1', name: 'A', ownerId: null } } },
]);
const result = await runAction(createItem, { input: { name: 'A' }, credential, fetch });
assert.deepEqual(result, { ok: true, items: [{ id: 'itm_1', name: 'A', ownerId: null }] });
assert.deepEqual(fetch.calls[0]?.body, { name: 'A' });
```

- `runAction(action, { input, credential, fetch })` runs the action through the executor
  n8n uses: it fills in defaults, validates the input, retries, and validates each item. An input value
  `''` counts as unset, as in n8n. It returns `{ ok: true, items }` or
  `{ ok: false, error: { message, path?, httpStatus? } }`. It does not throw.
- A route matches on `method` (default `GET`), the end of the URL path, and each listed `query` parameter
  (other parameters may also be present). When more routes match, the route with the most listed query
  parameters answers. `times: n` answers at most n calls, then the next matching route answers.
- `reply`: `{ status?, json?, binary?, headers? }`. `binary` is a body that is not JSON (base64 `data`). A call that no route matches fails with the list of routes.
- `fetch.calls[i]`: `{ method, url, path, query, headers, body }`. Query values are strings (a repeated
  key is an array), header names are lower case, a JSON body is already parsed, a byte body is text.
- Compare the whole result with `assert.deepEqual(result, ...)`: a failure then shows `result.error`.
- A run with more than 1000 mock calls fails: the code loops over the same page.
